  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1219 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThe upper low will continue lifting northeastward on Sunday,\nmaintaining (I'm going to sound like a broken record here) a\nthreat for showers and thunderstorms across much of eastern New\nMexico. By this time, drier air will begin infiltrating western\nportions of the CWA. Although storms will be a lot more\nprogressive as the system shifts east (storm motions around 40-50\nmph), continued high moisture content and modest instability will\nsupport instances of locally heavy rainfall, particularly across\nnortheastern New Mexico where at least 1-3 inches are forecast.\nIsolated strong to severe thunderstorms also remain possible\nacross southern and southeast New Mexico, with cool midlevel \ntemperatures and elongated hodographs suggesting that large hail\nwill once again be the primary concern. Snow may accumulate on the\nhighest peaks of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains through Sunday\nnight. Cooler temperatures are expected to persist through the\nweekend.\n\nBy Monday, while the low will still be lifting northeast into the\ncentral Plains, lingering southeasterly flow may sustain scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms across eastern New Mexico. However,\nPWATs are set to decrease to around 0.60-0.90 inches, reducing the\npotential for additional widespread rains and flash flooding\nconcerns. Drier condtions look take over by Tuesday and southwest\nflow sets in. As cloud cover and precipitation decreases,\ntemperatures will begin to moderate.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances could increase again Wednesday and\nThursday as another disturbance from the Pacific approaches the \nwest coast. However, long-term guidance continues to differ in \nsolutions regarding the ultimate track and timing of this system.\nConfidence is currently low, and the forecast will continue to be\nrefine in future packages.",
